1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:09,320
Okay, next section deals with thrombosis, and the idea in thrombosis is that we get pathologic formation of an intravascular blood clot.

2
00:00:09,320 --> 00:00:15,780
Now again, recall that the idea in the coagulation cascade was that when we had endothelial cell disruption,

3
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:21,940
and we had disruption to the blood vessel, that we formed a thrombus, which was sort of like a band-aid,

4
00:00:22,020 --> 00:00:24,300
which then would seal off that disruption.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,520
Now, in thrombosis, the problem is that instead of having a physiologic activation of the system,

6
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:35,180
we have some disorder that results in pathologic activation of the system,

7
00:00:35,180 --> 00:00:41,940
so that we start forming intravascular blood clot, or thrombus, but abnormally, at a time when we don't need it,

8
00:00:42,020 --> 00:00:48,260
and also within the blood vessel itself, which you don't want to normally seal off this blood vessel with a blood clot, obviously.

9
00:00:48,780 --> 00:00:51,720
Now, this can occur within an artery or a vein.

10
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:57,740
The most common location is the deep vein of the leg, usually below the knee, and that's called a DVT.

11
00:00:58,380 --> 00:01:00,140
We're all familiar with DVTs.

12
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:07,960
Now, it's important to note that when we have thrombus, it's characterized by lines of zon and attachment to the blood vessel wall.

13
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:09,340
Why is this important?

14
00:01:09,660 --> 00:01:17,540
Let's say that I'm looking at a patient at autopsy, and I see that in their coronary artery, there is a blood clot.

15
00:01:17,540 --> 00:01:24,020
And now I'm trying to decide, did that blood clot potentially kill the patient, because this is an autopsy, obviously.

16
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:31,300
So I could ask the question, did this blood clot occur before the patient died, or did it occur after the patient died?

17
00:01:31,540 --> 00:01:33,340
Now, how would it occur after the patient died?

18
00:01:33,580 --> 00:01:39,560
Because once blood is no longer moving around in the blood vessels, all of the blood is going to clot within the blood vessels,

19
00:01:39,560 --> 00:01:42,060
and so that would be an example of a post-mortem clot.

20
00:01:42,180 --> 00:01:45,420
Obviously, if the blood's not being pumped because the heart's not functioning anymore,

21
00:01:45,800 --> 00:01:48,820
you're going to get clotting of the blood throughout the entire vascular system.

22
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:53,840
So I could ask the question, did this blood clot occur before death or after death?

23
00:01:53,980 --> 00:01:59,520
And if it occurred before death, then I would have certain characteristics that I would be able to identify.

24
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:07,000
For example, in a thrombus, which would occur before death, you would see lines of zon, and you would see attachment to the vessel wall,

25
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:09,800
whereas you would not see these in a post-mortem clot.

26
00:02:10,300 --> 00:02:13,840
Now, that requires us to explain a little bit what are lines of zon.

27
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:18,900
So here's a picture of a thrombus that occurred, obviously, while the patient was alive,

28
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:22,460
and this particular thrombus has lines of zon.

29
00:02:22,460 --> 00:02:25,860
And what we mean by that is here are red blood cells, sort of this layer here,

30
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:29,440
and then you can see another layer of fibrin with platelets intermixed,

31
00:02:29,980 --> 00:02:34,760
and then another layer of red blood cells, and then another layer of platelet fibrin,

32
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:40,540
fibrin, and then another layer of red blood cells, another layer of platelet fibrin, etc.

33
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:42,480
And so those are the lines of zon.

34
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:45,640
Now, there are three major risk factors for thrombosis,

35
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:48,560
and if we understand the risk factors, we can then understand the disorders

36
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:52,020
that are going to increase the risk for thrombosis.

37
00:02:52,460 --> 00:02:55,240
The first of these risk factors is a disruption in blood flow.

38
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:57,840
The second is endothelial cell damage.

39
00:02:58,180 --> 00:03:00,740
And the third is a hypercoagulable state.

40
00:03:01,120 --> 00:03:03,380
And so we're going to talk about each of these in much more detail.

41
00:03:03,860 --> 00:03:10,440
Recall that these three, they basically form the classically described Verkau's triad.

42
00:03:10,440 --> 00:03:16,760
Well, let's discuss each one individually in order to highlight how thrombosis might develop.

43
00:03:17,400 --> 00:03:21,160
So the first of the risk factors for the development of thrombosis

44
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:23,140
is disruption in normal blood flow.

45
00:03:23,580 --> 00:03:25,480
Now, remember that when you look at a blood vessel,

46
00:03:25,580 --> 00:03:28,740
so that's the wall of the blood vessel lined by endothelium, correct?

47
00:03:29,120 --> 00:03:36,640
As blood flows through the blood vessel, it flows in a laminar pattern, in a layered pattern.

48
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:42,400
And it's that layered pattern that helps to keep factors dispersed and inactivated.

49
00:03:42,400 --> 00:03:45,880
Now, if there's stasis so that the blood is no longer flowing,

50
00:03:46,320 --> 00:03:48,900
then that would increase the risk for development of thrombus.

51
00:03:49,380 --> 00:03:53,840
Or if there's turbulence so that the blood is no longer flowing in this laminar pattern,

52
00:03:54,140 --> 00:03:56,320
that would also increase the risk for thrombosis.

53
00:03:56,560 --> 00:03:58,120
Let's talk about some examples.

54
00:03:58,560 --> 00:04:01,840
For example, immobilization increases the risk for thrombosis.

55
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:07,120
And we're well aware of the fact that there's an increased risk of DVT if the patient's not moving around.

56
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:12,160
That's because there's stasis of the blood, and when there's stasis of the blood,

57
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:16,440
there can be activation of the coag cascade, which can then result in a thrombus.

58
00:04:16,840 --> 00:04:19,140
Another example would be cardiac wall dysfunction.

59
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:21,320
For example, let's say that there's an arrhythmia.

60
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:25,280
The patient has AFib, so that the atrium is no longer moving properly.

61
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:29,000
If the atrium is not moving properly, then blood is not going to move properly.

62
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:32,240
And blood is going to become, quote-unquote, slightly static,

63
00:04:32,340 --> 00:04:35,640
or it will not be moving along as it should.

64
00:04:35,640 --> 00:04:38,680
And that will increase the risk of formation of thrombus as well.

65
00:04:38,760 --> 00:04:44,840
And we know that in patients with AFib, one of the complications is that they get thrombus within their atrium.

66
00:04:45,420 --> 00:04:48,940
Another example would be myocardial infarction, where the patient gets an infarction

67
00:04:48,940 --> 00:04:54,200
so that they're not able to move the wall of the muscle very well, the cardiac muscle.

68
00:04:54,340 --> 00:04:56,020
So the cardiac wall is not moving well,

69
00:04:56,140 --> 00:05:00,200
which would then mean that blood could potentially remain static around that area,

70
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:02,720
which would increase the risk of thrombosis as well.

71
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:05,700
And the third example I've given you here would be aneurysm.

72
00:05:05,780 --> 00:05:06,780
Now, what's an aneurysm?

73
00:05:07,080 --> 00:05:10,900
An aneurysm is a balloon-like dilatation of a portion of the blood vessel.

74
00:05:11,180 --> 00:05:16,420
So pretend this is a blood vessel, and then here we come into a balloon-like dilatation of the blood vessel here.

75
00:05:16,760 --> 00:05:19,480
Now, normally the blood flow is laminar and continuous,

76
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:26,080
but when the blood goes into this area of aneurysm, it can become slightly irregular in its flow.

77
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:32,400
And when it becomes irregular in its flow, there's going to be an increased risk for formation of thrombus within the aneurysm.

78
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:36,100
So that's an important general principle of pathology as well.

79
00:05:37,040 --> 00:05:39,840
The next of the risk factors is endothelial damage.

80
00:05:40,060 --> 00:05:43,820
And the reason why endothelial damage increases the risk for thrombosis

81
00:05:43,820 --> 00:05:48,660
is because the endothelium is highly protective against the formation of thrombus.

82
00:05:49,100 --> 00:05:54,860
The endothelium does many things to prohibit the activation of the coagulation cascade.

83
00:05:54,860 --> 00:05:58,420
And it's worth taking a little bit of time because each of these are high-yield.

84
00:05:58,520 --> 00:06:01,360
And by the way, they're detailed in your book, so you don't need to write.

85
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:04,000
You can, if you just pay attention and listen to what I'm saying,

86
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:06,960
you can always go back and look at the details over there.

87
00:06:07,300 --> 00:06:13,040
So let's give some examples of what the endothelium does to protect against the formation of thrombosis.

88
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:17,240
Now, the first way by which the endothelium protects against thrombosis

89
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:24,660
is by simply forming a barrier that blocks away subendothelial collagen and the underlying tissue factor.

90
00:06:24,860 --> 00:06:28,120
Remember that subendothelial collagen, when it's exposed,

91
00:06:28,360 --> 00:06:34,880
will then allow the platelets to stick or adhese to the area of disruption.

92
00:06:35,400 --> 00:06:37,300
And then that allows the platelets to aggregate,

93
00:06:37,540 --> 00:06:44,240
which then subsequently allows for the coagulation cascade to have a surface upon which it can be activated.

94
00:06:44,640 --> 00:06:48,920
So if you block the subendothelial collagen and the underlying tissue factor,

95
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:50,540
that protects against thrombosis.

96
00:06:50,540 --> 00:06:54,420
So that's one way by which the endothelium protects against thrombosis.

97
00:06:55,000 --> 00:07:00,440
Another way is that the endothelium produces molecules like prostaglandin I2.

98
00:07:02,520 --> 00:07:06,040
Prostaglandin I2, it blocks platelet aggregation.

99
00:07:06,340 --> 00:07:08,300
It's sort of the opposite of thromboxane E2.

100
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:13,200
So the platelets produce thromboxane E2, but the endothelium produces prostaglandin I2,

101
00:07:13,300 --> 00:07:14,900
and these are sort of opposite of one another.

102
00:07:14,900 --> 00:07:19,280
That's one way by which the endothelium can block platelet aggregation

103
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:22,800
and hence block the activation of the coagulation cascade and thrombosis.

104
00:07:23,260 --> 00:07:25,320
Another way would be to produce nitric oxide.

105
00:07:25,460 --> 00:07:32,640
Nitric oxide causes vasodilation, which then protects against activation of the coagulation cascade as well.

106
00:07:32,640 --> 00:07:39,960
Another example is that the endothelium secretes molecules that are called heparin-like molecules.

107
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:45,980
So if this was the endothelium, for example, one of the molecules that's secreted are heparin-like molecules.

108
00:07:46,480 --> 00:07:50,660
Heparin-like molecules, they activate an enzyme called antithrombin-3.

109
00:07:51,120 --> 00:07:53,100
And guess what antithrombin-3 does?

110
00:07:53,780 --> 00:07:56,100
Antithrombin, so it inactivates thrombin.

111
00:07:56,100 --> 00:08:03,140
And remember that thrombin is the key molecule generated by the coag cascade that would then allow for cross-linking of the clot.

112
00:08:03,260 --> 00:08:07,520
So this particular molecule, antithrombin-3, is going to get rid of thrombin.

113
00:08:07,860 --> 00:08:10,620
And it's also going to get rid of the things that form thrombin.

114
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:13,720
And what forms thrombin is the coagulation cascade.

115
00:08:13,900 --> 00:08:17,740
And so many of the coagulation factors will be disrupted by antithrombin-3.

116
00:08:18,300 --> 00:08:24,800
So again, the production of heparin-like molecules by the endothelium allows for activation of antithrombin-3,

117
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:31,120
which then produces an anticoagulant effect or an antithrombotic effect.

118
00:08:32,120 --> 00:08:38,760
Another example of a molecule produced by the endothelium is TPA, tissue plasminogen activator.

119
00:08:38,920 --> 00:08:41,000
So the endothelium produces TPA.

120
00:08:41,600 --> 00:08:45,580
And it's important because remember that TPA converts plasminogen to plasmin.

121
00:08:45,980 --> 00:08:48,220
And remember that plasmin has three functions.

122
00:08:48,660 --> 00:08:53,560
Number one, plasmin cleaves fibrin, where a clot to be present.

123
00:08:53,560 --> 00:08:58,500
Or it can also cleave serum fibrinogen to ensure that a clot won't form.

124
00:08:58,940 --> 00:09:05,120
So that's one way by which plasmin activates, plasmin blocks the formation of thrombus.

125
00:09:05,560 --> 00:09:08,980
Another way by which plasmin does this is to destroy coagulation factors.

126
00:09:09,120 --> 00:09:12,340
Remember that's the second mechanism of plasmin function.

127
00:09:12,780 --> 00:09:16,540
And the third way is that plasmin can block platelet aggregation.

128
00:09:16,540 --> 00:09:21,360
Now all of these things, the cleaving of serum fibrinogen and cleaving of fibrin,

129
00:09:21,420 --> 00:09:26,100
where a clot to be present, the destruction of coagulation factors and the blocking of platelet aggregation,

130
00:09:26,480 --> 00:09:31,320
all of these things are essential for protecting against the formation of clot.

131
00:09:31,900 --> 00:09:34,660
So again, the endothelium is doing a very important job.

132
00:09:34,660 --> 00:09:41,120
And the final molecule that I want to discuss that's secreted by the endothelium is something called thrombomodulin.

133
00:09:41,640 --> 00:09:43,300
Now what does thrombomodulin do?

134
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:48,620
Thrombomodulin takes thrombin and modulates its activity to do something else.

135
00:09:49,020 --> 00:09:50,500
Now remember, what does thrombin do?

136
00:09:50,600 --> 00:09:55,340
Thrombin is the product of the coagulation cascade that converts fibrinogen to fibrin,

137
00:09:55,420 --> 00:10:01,040
which then allows for cross-linking of the fibrin to produce the stable platelet fibrin thrombus.

138
00:10:01,660 --> 00:10:04,560
Now in this particular case, what happens is that thrombomodulin,

139
00:10:05,080 --> 00:10:10,120
what it does is it takes thrombin and instead of allowing thrombin to convert fibrinogen to fibrin,

140
00:10:10,440 --> 00:10:13,840
it modulates the activity of thrombin, hence the term thrombomodulin,

141
00:10:14,200 --> 00:10:16,800
so that thrombin can actually activate protein C.

142
00:10:17,220 --> 00:10:18,520
Now what does protein C do?

143
00:10:18,520 --> 00:10:21,520
It then inactivates factors 5 and factor 8,

144
00:10:21,820 --> 00:10:25,720
which are important amplifying factors within the coagulation cascade.

145
00:10:26,200 --> 00:10:27,540
So this is important as well.

146
00:10:27,540 --> 00:10:31,700
And that is that the endothelium produces thrombomodulin,

147
00:10:31,860 --> 00:10:34,540
which is then going to redirect the function of thrombin.

148
00:10:35,480 --> 00:10:39,760
Anyway, all of these are protective effects of the endothelial cell.

149
00:10:40,040 --> 00:10:43,780
And if the endothelial cell is damaged, as I'm trying to highlight here,

150
00:10:44,040 --> 00:10:46,620
there's going to be an increased risk for thrombosis.

151
00:10:47,000 --> 00:10:48,420
So that's the second risk factor.

152
00:10:48,940 --> 00:10:53,660
And some examples of endothelial damage include atherosclerosis.

153
00:10:53,660 --> 00:10:57,740
When there is atherosclerosis, that results in endothelial damage.

154
00:10:57,980 --> 00:11:02,600
And recall that atherosclerosis is a major risk factor for the development of thrombosis.

155
00:11:03,680 --> 00:11:07,000
Vasculitis, when patients get vasculitis, they get damage to the endothelium.

156
00:11:07,340 --> 00:11:09,420
And that can also increase the risk for thrombosis.

157
00:11:09,540 --> 00:11:12,740
And we'll hear more about that when we do the blood vessel lecture.

158
00:11:12,740 --> 00:11:19,320
And then finally, high levels of serum homocysteine can also cause endothelial damage.

159
00:11:19,720 --> 00:11:24,100
And this is a little high yield because it goes back to biochemistry and examiners like to go after it.

160
00:11:24,300 --> 00:11:25,960
So I just want to touch on this briefly.

161
00:11:26,240 --> 00:11:29,960
There's a couple ways by which we can get elevated levels of homocysteine.

162
00:11:30,340 --> 00:11:33,920
One way would be to develop a vitamin B12 or folate deficiency.

163
00:11:34,480 --> 00:11:38,200
Now the idea here is that when patients become vitamin B12 or folate deficient,

164
00:11:38,200 --> 00:11:42,240
they can no longer convert homocysteine to methionine.

165
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:48,780
Recall from biochemistry that folate, when it comes into the body as tetrahydrofolate,

166
00:11:49,180 --> 00:11:52,800
so let's call that THF, tetrahydrofolate, it gets methylated.

167
00:11:53,300 --> 00:11:56,820
And in order for tetrahydrofolate to participate in DNA synthesis,

168
00:11:57,600 --> 00:11:59,380
it needs to lose that methyl group.

169
00:11:59,480 --> 00:12:02,500
So what it does is it gives that methyl group to vitamin B12.

170
00:12:03,160 --> 00:12:05,520
Vitamin B12, of course, accepts that methyl group.

171
00:12:05,520 --> 00:12:09,120
And so now tetrahydrofolate can participate in DNA synthesis.

172
00:12:09,620 --> 00:12:14,620
Vitamin B12 then quickly hands off that methyl group to a molecule called homocysteine.

173
00:12:15,940 --> 00:12:19,320
And then homocysteine eventually will become methionine.

174
00:12:21,120 --> 00:12:26,100
And imagine that if we don't have folate or if we don't have vitamin B12,

175
00:12:26,400 --> 00:12:31,520
we won't be able to obviously hand off that methyl group to homocysteine to produce methionine,

176
00:12:31,620 --> 00:12:33,480
so homocysteine will increase.

177
00:12:33,480 --> 00:12:37,480
And when homocysteine increases, that damages the endothelium,

178
00:12:38,520 --> 00:12:40,480
giving an increased risk for thrombosis.

179
00:12:41,480 --> 00:12:44,480
So one way by which patients can have an increased risk of thrombosis

180
00:12:45,060 --> 00:12:48,260
is to have a vitamin B12 or folate deficiency.

181
00:12:49,200 --> 00:12:54,600
Another way by which this could occur is that we could have a deficiency in an enzyme

182
00:12:54,600 --> 00:12:59,480
that's necessary to convert homocysteine to cystathionine.

183
00:12:59,480 --> 00:13:04,480
And that enzyme is called CBS or cystathionine beta synthase.

184
00:13:04,480 --> 00:13:10,480
Now the idea here is that patients who have a genetic deficiency in this particular enzyme,

185
00:13:10,480 --> 00:13:12,480
they can't convert homocysteine to cystathionine.

186
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:17,480
And if they can't convert it to cystathionine, then that's going to result in high homocysteine levels.

187
00:13:17,480 --> 00:13:24,480
And those high homocysteine levels will produce a disorder called homocysteine.

188
00:13:24,480 --> 00:13:28,480
Now remember that in homocysteine, go back to genetics,

189
00:13:28,480 --> 00:13:30,480
these patients have a few important features.

190
00:13:30,480 --> 00:13:33,480
Like they get vessel thrombosis, but we understand why.

191
00:13:33,480 --> 00:13:38,480
The high serum homocysteine will actually then result in endothelial damage,

192
00:13:38,480 --> 00:13:41,480
which will then allow for thrombosis to occur.

193
00:13:41,480 --> 00:13:46,480
And in fact, they think about a fourth of these patients actually die at a young age due to thrombosis.

194
00:13:46,480 --> 00:13:49,480
These patients also have mental retardation.

195
00:13:49,480 --> 00:13:54,480
They get dislocation of the lens, and they have these long slender fingers as well.

196
00:13:54,480 --> 00:14:01,480
And so these are some of the features that we might see in homocysteine area due to a CBS deficiency.

197
00:14:01,480 --> 00:14:10,480
Okay, and then the third way by which we can get thrombosis, or I should say the third risk factor,

198
00:14:10,480 --> 00:14:12,480
is a hypercoagulable state.

199
00:14:12,480 --> 00:14:15,480
Now the idea here is that in a hypercoagulable state,

200
00:14:15,480 --> 00:14:21,480
the balance between procoagulants and anticoagulants that's normally present within the blood

201
00:14:21,480 --> 00:14:27,480
for the coagulation system to remain functional only when necessary, that balance is upset.

202
00:14:27,480 --> 00:14:30,480
So remember that we normally have procoagulant proteins that allow for coagulation,

203
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:36,480
and we have anticoagulant proteins that stop the coagulation, and we need that balance.

204
00:14:36,480 --> 00:14:41,480
So if we have too many procoagulant proteins, that will result in a hypercoagulable state,

205
00:14:41,480 --> 00:14:43,480
giving us a risk for thrombosis.

206
00:14:43,480 --> 00:14:48,480
And if we lack the anticoagulant proteins, that would also give us an imbalance,

207
00:14:48,480 --> 00:14:51,480
resulting in an increased risk for thrombosis.

208
00:14:51,480 --> 00:14:54,480
Now of course these disorders can be inherited or acquired,

209
00:14:54,480 --> 00:14:56,480
and we'll see that as we move forward.

210
00:14:56,480 --> 00:14:59,480
When patients have a hypercoagulable state,

211
00:14:59,480 --> 00:15:03,480
they tend to present with recurrent DVTs or DVTs at a young age.

212
00:15:03,480 --> 00:15:06,480
So again, usually this occurs in the deep veins of the legs.

213
00:15:06,480 --> 00:15:13,480
Other sites where they could have recurrent thrombosis would include the hepatic veins and the cerebral vein.

214
00:15:13,480 --> 00:15:16,480
So these are some other sites to be aware of.

215
00:15:16,480 --> 00:15:22,480
The first example of such a hypercoagulable state would be a lack of protein C or protein S.

216
00:15:22,480 --> 00:15:27,480
Now remember that protein C and protein S, what they do is they inactivate factors 5 and 8.

217
00:15:27,480 --> 00:15:28,480
That's the normal.

218
00:15:28,480 --> 00:15:35,480
So protein C and protein S normally inactivate factors 5 and 8, and factors 5 and 8 are amplifying factors in the coagulation cascade.

219
00:15:35,480 --> 00:15:42,480
So you do want to be able to inactivate them so you can shut down the cascade when you want it to be turned off.

220
00:15:42,480 --> 00:15:48,480
Now if you don't have protein C or protein S, you get less negative feedback on the coagulation cascade,

221
00:15:48,480 --> 00:15:51,480
and the patients become hypercoagulable.

222
00:15:51,480 --> 00:15:57,480
And so protein C or protein S deficiency will increase the risk for a hypercoagulable state.

223
00:15:57,480 --> 00:16:02,480
Now an interesting thing that can occur in these patients, and something that's a feared complication,

224
00:16:02,480 --> 00:16:08,480
is that there's an increased risk for warfarin or coumadin skin necrosis.

225
00:16:08,480 --> 00:16:16,480
And this is a high yield discussion, so let's take a moment to actually remind ourselves of what's going on in this particular circumstance.

226
00:16:16,480 --> 00:16:20,480
Now remember that when we give patients coumadin or warfarin,

227
00:16:20,480 --> 00:16:24,480
what we're really doing is blocking epoxide reductase within the liver.

228
00:16:24,480 --> 00:16:27,480
That then blocks the ability to activate vitamin K.

229
00:16:27,480 --> 00:16:37,480
And if you can't activate vitamin K, then remember that factors 2, 7, 9, 10, protein C and protein S

230
00:16:37,480 --> 00:16:42,480
will no longer be able to be functioning properly or produced properly.

231
00:16:42,480 --> 00:16:48,480
Now you've got a patient that basically is sitting around with normal levels of all of these factors, right?

232
00:16:48,480 --> 00:16:52,480
And then what you do is you give them coumadin or warfarin,

233
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:55,480
and you start to decrease the production of new factors.

234
00:16:55,480 --> 00:17:02,480
So you're not going to be able to produce any more new 2, new 7, new 9, new 10, new C, or new S.

235
00:17:02,480 --> 00:17:06,480
So what's then going to happen is that these factors are going to start degrading

236
00:17:06,480 --> 00:17:08,480
because the old factors were present in the blood.

237
00:17:08,480 --> 00:17:11,480
And so for a period of time, the old factors will still be present.

238
00:17:11,480 --> 00:17:15,480
Now it so happens that the very first of these factors to actually degrade,

239
00:17:15,480 --> 00:17:20,480
the one that has the shortest half-life, are protein C and protein S.

240
00:17:20,480 --> 00:17:22,480
And so these will be the initial ones to degrade.

241
00:17:22,480 --> 00:17:28,480
And when these degrade, you're going to have these factors around without the anticoagulant factors.

242
00:17:28,480 --> 00:17:34,480
And so without those anticoagulant factors, there's going to be an increased risk for activation of these factors,

243
00:17:34,480 --> 00:17:38,480
so there's an increased risk for the formation of thrombus.

244
00:17:38,480 --> 00:17:44,480
And that's why when patients are put on coumadin, we make sure that we also have heparin running at the same time

245
00:17:44,480 --> 00:17:49,480
because by having heparin running at the same time, we protect them for that window.

246
00:17:49,480 --> 00:17:50,480
And what is that window?

247
00:17:50,480 --> 00:17:56,480
The window period until these factors will also be destroyed over time, right?

248
00:17:56,480 --> 00:18:00,480
Because they also have a half-life that will be relatively short.

249
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:05,480
And so over time, these factors would also be destroyed, and then we can remove the heparin.

250
00:18:05,480 --> 00:18:09,480
Now what happens in patients with protein CRS deficiency is that you've got this patient

251
00:18:09,480 --> 00:18:12,480
that's got all these normal factors around, right?

252
00:18:12,480 --> 00:18:16,480
And as these normal factors are present, then you give them coumadin.

253
00:18:16,480 --> 00:18:20,480
Now if these patients were already deficient in CNS, and you give them coumadin,

254
00:18:20,480 --> 00:18:27,480
they're now really going to be very quickly deficient in CNS because if there's a little bit of CNS around,

255
00:18:27,480 --> 00:18:31,480
now that is also going to be gone with the short half-life.

256
00:18:31,480 --> 00:18:35,480
And so now there's a very increased risk of developing a hypercoagulable state.

257
00:18:35,480 --> 00:18:39,480
And so these patients have an increased risk were they to get this hypercoagulable state

258
00:18:39,480 --> 00:18:44,480
of getting thrombosis within the skin, which is called warfarin skin necrosis.

259
00:18:44,480 --> 00:18:48,480
So that's protein C or protein S deficiency.

260
00:18:48,480 --> 00:18:51,480
The next disorder that we want to discuss is factor V Leiden.

261
00:18:51,480 --> 00:18:56,480
And basically this is a very interesting disorder in which the patients have a mutated form of factor V.

262
00:18:56,480 --> 00:19:01,480
Now remember that factor V is able to be shut off by protein C and protein S.

263
00:19:01,480 --> 00:19:05,480
And the way that protein C and protein S shut off factor V is they go there and they cleave it.

264
00:19:05,480 --> 00:19:11,480
Now in factor V Leiden, the problem is that you've got a mutated factor V that can't be cleaved.

265
00:19:11,480 --> 00:19:15,480
It lacks the cleavage site for deactivation by protein C and protein S.

266
00:19:15,480 --> 00:19:20,480
So this then results in an inability to shut off factor V.

267
00:19:20,480 --> 00:19:25,480
And that inability to shut off factor V leads to excessive factor V activity,

268
00:19:25,480 --> 00:19:28,480
which then can lead to a hypercoagulable state.

269
00:19:28,480 --> 00:19:32,480
And this is the most common inherited cause of a hypercoagulable state.

270
00:19:32,480 --> 00:19:38,480
Another example that can result in a hypercoagulable state is prothrombin 2210A.

271
00:19:38,480 --> 00:19:46,480
In this particular case, what happens is you've got a point mutation in the prothrombin gene that results in excess production of prothrombin.

272
00:19:46,480 --> 00:19:48,480
And guess what prothrombin is?

273
00:19:48,480 --> 00:19:49,480
It's prothrombin.

274
00:19:49,480 --> 00:19:51,480
It advances the effect of thrombin.

275
00:19:51,480 --> 00:19:55,480
And thrombin's goal is to convert fibrinogen to fibrin.

276
00:19:55,480 --> 00:20:02,480
So this is going to promote thrombus formation, which again would increase the risk for a hypercoagulable state.

277
00:20:02,480 --> 00:20:08,480
Antithrombin 3 deficiency is a disorder in which the patients lack antithrombin 3.

278
00:20:08,480 --> 00:20:13,480
So remember that the endothelial cell is normally making heparin-like molecules.

279
00:20:13,480 --> 00:20:18,480
And those heparin-like molecules are binding antithrombin 3 and they activate antithrombin 3

280
00:20:18,480 --> 00:20:26,480
so that antithrombin 3 can then inactivate thrombin and can also inactivate coagulation factors that could lead to the production of thrombin.

281
00:20:26,480 --> 00:20:32,480
And so if there's an antithrombin 3 deficiency, these heparin-like molecules aren't going to be able to activate antithrombin 3.

282
00:20:32,480 --> 00:20:37,480
And so that'll increase the risk for formation of thrombus.

283
00:20:37,480 --> 00:20:45,480
One of the very interesting things in patients with antithrombin 3 deficiency is that if you give them heparin at a standard dose, the PTT will not rise.

284
00:20:45,480 --> 00:20:52,480
First of all, remember that when you give heparin, you expect the PTT to rise and that's how you're going to monitor the heparin.

285
00:20:52,480 --> 00:20:57,480
By the way, the PTT would also rise, but you use the PTT to monitor the heparin.

286
00:20:57,480 --> 00:20:59,480
So that's the first point.

287
00:20:59,480 --> 00:21:03,480
Now in this particular deficiency, when you give heparin, the PTT does not rise.

288
00:21:03,480 --> 00:21:04,480
And why does it not rise?

289
00:21:04,480 --> 00:21:08,480
Because remember that heparin works by activating antithrombin 3.

290
00:21:08,480 --> 00:21:09,480
That's the way that heparin works.

291
00:21:09,480 --> 00:21:13,480
You give heparin and then it binds and activates antithrombin 3.

292
00:21:13,480 --> 00:21:21,480
So in this particular case, when you gave heparin, there would be no antithrombin 3 and so there would be no effect of the heparin and so the PTT would not rise.

293
00:21:21,480 --> 00:21:24,480
Then what you do is you give the patient high doses of heparin.

294
00:21:24,480 --> 00:21:35,480
That activates the limited antithrombin 3 that's present, which then allows the patient to go into a certain degree of an anticoagulated state.

295
00:21:35,480 --> 00:21:41,480
You then give coumadin, which would then be given to maintain the anticoagulated state.

296
00:21:41,480 --> 00:21:47,480
And after you pass the window in which you would worry about coumadin skin necrosis, you can of course stop the high dose heparin.

297
00:21:47,480 --> 00:21:52,480
So this is another important example to be aware of.

298
00:21:52,480 --> 00:21:56,480
Finally, I'd like to say that oral contraceptives are associated with a hypercoagulable state.

299
00:21:56,480 --> 00:22:04,480
The reason why is that estrogen induces increased production of coagulation factors and that would then increase the risk for thrombosis as well.

300
00:22:04,480 --> 00:22:10,480
And that closes off our section on thrombosis and we're now going to do the final section, which is embolism.

301
00:22:10,480 --> 00:22:12,480
this is embolism.

